","widget":{"name":"select_every_nth"},"link":null},{"localized_name":"format","name":"format","shape":7,"type":"COMBO","widget":{"name":"format"},"link":null}],"outputs":[{"localized_name":"IMAGE","name":"IMAGE","type":"IMAGE","links":null},{"localized_name":"frame_count","name":"frame_count","type":"INT","links":null},{"localized_name":"audio","name":"audio","type":"AUDIO","links":null},{"localized_name":"video_info","name":"video_info","type":"VHS_VIDEOINFO","links":null}],"properties":{"cnr_id":"comfyui-videohelpersuite","ver":"1.7.9","Node name for S&R":"VHS_LoadVideoPath"},"widgets_values":{"video":"","force_rate":0,"custom_width":0,"custom_height":0,"frame_load_cap":0,"skip_first_frames":0,"select_every_nth":1,"format":"AnimateDiff","videopreview":{"hidden":false,"paused":false,"params":{"filename":"","type":"path","format":"video/","force_rate":0,"custom_width":0,"custom_height":0,"frame_load_cap":0,"skip_first_frames":0,"select_every_nth":1}}},"widgets_values_named":{"video":"","force_rate":0,"custom_width":0,"custom_height":0,"frame_load_cap":0,"skip_first_frames":0,"select_every_nth":1,"format":"AnimateDiff","videopreview":{"hidden":false,"paused":false,"params":{"filename":"","type":"path","format":"video/","force_rate":0,"custom_width":0,"custom_height":0,"frame_load_cap":0,"skip_first_frames":0,"select_every_nth":1}}}},{"id":6,"type":"Note","pos":[-227.01296637524396,506.30172428961487],"size":[523.4606837295138,151.4234535867904],"flags":{},"order":3,"mode":0,"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"properties":{},"widgets_values":["NOTES: \n\nIf your input video is too long / too large, try the Load Video (Path) node instead and point to the input video's path.\n\nWith the VHS video input nodes, simply pass the video_info output to the video_info input of the [SHARP VR Video Batch] node, to pass both the audio track (if exists) and the FPS of the video to the Video Combine node at the end."],"widgets_values_named":{"text":"NOTES: \n\nIf your input video is too long / too large, try the Load Video (Path) node instead and point to the input video's path.\n\nWith the VHS video input nodes, simply pass the video_info output to the video_info input of the [SHARP VR Video Batch] node, to pass both the audio track (if exists) and the FPS of the video to the Video Combine node at the end."},"color":"#432","bgcolor":"#653"},{"id":7,"type":"Note","pos":[414.2832208759676,691.2153894949195],"size":[501.8358396853939,327.44388150659506],"flags":{},"order":4,"mode":0,"inputs":[],"outputs":[],"properties":{},"widgets_values":["BASIC GUIDE:\n\n* I'd keep focal length 35mm (works well most footage).\n\n* For custom_baseline_mm, enter your IPD ideally, for most people its somewhere between 50-75mm.\n\n* custom_convergence_m dictates how close/far the objects in the scene feel to your eyes, in VR i find 3 meters (3.0 here) to feel best for stereoscopy 3D effect of shots with people. Too low = eye-strain. Too high = weaker 3D effect.\n\n* You can dolly in a bit closer to the center of the scene with the camera_dolly_m value. I find +0.05 m to be a meaningful limit, too much dolly-in and you're just getting too close and clipping into geometry of the center objects.\n\n* You can orbit off-center around the center of the scene left/right/higher/lower with the two camera_orbit_* values. +yaw = orbiting to higher than center, -yaw = orbiting to lower than center, +pitch = orbiting to the right side of center, -pitch = orbiting to the left side of center. I find +/-1.0 degree in any orbit axis to be a meaningful limit, too much orbiting off-center and you start seeing a ton of gaussian splatting reconstruction artifacts.\n\nTry the Dolly/orbit motion nodes! Should be fun.\n\nThe rest you can keep default, or fiddle around."],"widgets_values_named":{"text":"BASIC GUIDE:\n\n* I'd keep focal length 35mm (works well most footage).\n\n* For custom_baseline_mm, enter your IPD ideally, for most people its somewhere between 50-75mm.\n\n* custom_convergence_m dictates how close/far the objects in the scene feel to your eyes, in VR i find 3 meters (3.0 here) to feel best for stereoscopy 3D effect of shots with people. Too low = eye-strain. Too high = weaker 3D effect.\n\n* You can dolly in a bit closer to the center of the scene with the camera_dolly_m value. I find +0.05 m to be a meaningful limit, too much dolly-in and you're just getting too close and clipping into geometry of the center objects.\n\n* You can orbit off-center around the center of the scene left/right/higher/lower with the two camera_orbit_* values. +yaw = orbiting to higher than center, -yaw = orbiting to lower than center, +pitch = orbiting to the right side of center, -pitch = orbiting to the left side of center. I find +/-1.0 degree in any orbit axis to be a meaningful limit, too much orbiting off-center and you start seeing a ton of gaussian splatting reconstruction artifacts.\n\nTry the Dolly/orbit motion nodes! Should be fun.\n\nThe rest you can keep default, or fiddle around."},"color":"#432","bgcolor":"#653"}],"links":[[1,1,0,3,0,"SHARP_VR_VIDEO_MODEL_CONFIG"],[2,2,0,3,1,"IMAGE"],[3,2,3,3,2,"VHS_VIDEOINFO"],[4,3,0,4,0,"IMAGE"],[5,3,1,4,1,"AUDIO"],[6,3,2,4,4,"FLOAT"]],"groups":[],"config":{},"extra":{"ds":{"scale":0.8472426733466802,"offset":[660.5761750154882,276.23676303786317]}},"version":0.4}